    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Meat</span> is the flesh of animals used as food. It is one of the richest sources of first-class protein in the diet and a major focus of practical cookery in Food and Nutrition. Understanding meat types, structure, and cooking methods helps in planning nutritious, well-prepared meals.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Meat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Meat refers to the edible flesh of animals, including muscle, fat, and connective tissue, eaten as food. It is classified according to the animal source:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Red meat</td><td>Mammals</td><td>Beef, goat meat, mutton, pork</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>White meat</td><td>Birds and some young animals</td><td>Chicken, turkey, veal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Game meat</td><td>Wild animals</td><td>Bush meat, venison, rabbit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Offal (organ meat)</td><td>Internal organs</td><td>Liver, kidney, heart, tripe</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of Meat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Meat is made up of <strong>muscle fibres</strong> bound together by <strong>connective tissue</strong>, with <strong>fat</strong> deposited within and around the muscle, and permeated by blood vessels. The proportion of connective tissue determines toughness — meat from well-exercised parts (like the leg) has more connective tissue and is tougher than meat from less-used parts (like the loin).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutritive Value of Meat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Meat supplies first-class (complete) protein containing all essential amino acids, fat, iron, zinc, and B-vitamins (especially B12). It contains little or no carbohydrate and no dietary fibre.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Factors Affecting Choice of Meat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When choosing meat, consider: freshness (colour, smell, texture), age of the animal (younger meat is more tender), cut required for the dish, cost, family preference, and intended cooking method.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Methods of Cooking Meat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Meat is cooked using <strong>moist methods</strong> (stewing, boiling, braising, pressure cooking — suited to tough cuts with more connective tissue) and <strong>dry methods</strong> (grilling, roasting, frying — suited to tender cuts). Slow moist cooking breaks down connective tissue (collagen) into gelatine, tenderising tough meat.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common WAEC question asks why tough cuts of meat are cooked by moist methods. Remember: prolonged moist heat converts collagen (connective tissue) into gelatine, making the meat tender.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Meat is classified as red, white, game, or offal; it is a rich source of first-class protein, fat, iron and B-vitamins. Tough cuts (high connective tissue) need moist cooking; tender cuts suit dry cooking.\n</div>\n",
