    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Poultry</span> refers to domesticated birds reared for their meat and eggs. It is one of the most affordable and widely consumed sources of animal protein in Nigeria, and understanding its structure, selection, and preparation is essential in Food and Nutrition.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Poultry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Poultry means domestic birds kept for food. Common types include chicken, turkey, duck, guinea fowl, and goose. Chicken is the most widely eaten poultry in Nigeria.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of Poultry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Like meat, poultry consists of muscle fibres, connective tissue, fat and skin. However, poultry generally has less connective tissue than red meat, making it more tender and quicker to cook.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Preparation of Poultry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Preparation involves plucking (removing feathers), singeing (removing fine hairs over a flame), drawing/eviscerating (removing internal organs), washing, and jointing (cutting into pieces) before cooking.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Nutritive Value of Poultry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Poultry is a good source of first-class protein, is generally lower in fat than red meat (especially the breast, without skin), and supplies B-vitamins and minerals such as phosphorus.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Factors Affecting Choice and Cooking Methods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Choice depends on freshness, age (young birds are more tender), size needed, and cost. Because poultry is naturally tender, it can be cooked by both dry methods (roasting, grilling, frying) and moist methods (stewing, boiling), unlike tough cuts of red meat which need moist cooking.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Plucking</td><td>Remove feathers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Singeing</td><td>Remove fine hairs/down using flame</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Drawing</td><td>Remove internal organs (evisceration)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Washing</td><td>Clean the carcass thoroughly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Jointing</td><td>Cut into serving pieces</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often tests the correct order of poultry preparation: plucking → singeing → drawing → washing → jointing. Learn this sequence carefully.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Poultry (chicken, turkey, duck, guinea fowl) is a tender, affordable protein source prepared through plucking, singeing, drawing, washing and jointing, and can be cooked by both dry and moist methods.\n</div>\n",
