    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Eggs</span> are a versatile, nutrient-dense food produced by birds, most commonly the hen. They are used both as a standalone dish and as an ingredient in many other recipes, making their structure, quality testing, and cookery important topics.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Uses of Eggs</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An egg is the reproductive body laid by a female bird, widely eaten as food. Eggs are used for direct dishes (boiled, fried, poached) and as functional ingredients — binding (in meatballs), aerating (in cakes), thickening (in custards), coating (in fried foods), glazing (pastries), and emulsifying (mayonnaise).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of an Egg</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Description/Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Shell</td><td>Hard, porous outer covering; protects contents; allows air exchange</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Air cell</td><td>Space at the broad end; enlarges as the egg ages</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Albumen (white)</td><td>Clear protein fluid surrounding the yolk; thick and thin layers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Yolk</td><td>Yellow central part; contains fat, protein, vitamins A, D, E and iron</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chalazae</td><td>Twisted cord-like strands that hold the yolk centred in the egg</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitelline membrane</td><td>Thin membrane surrounding the yolk</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutritive Value of Eggs</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Eggs supply first-class protein, fat (mostly in the yolk), vitamins A, D, E and B12, and iron. They contain very little carbohydrate and no dietary fibre or vitamin C.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Test for Freshness</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Common freshness tests include: the <strong>water/salt-solution test</strong> (a fresh egg sinks and lies flat; a stale egg floats due to a larger air cell), the <strong>candling test</strong> (holding the egg to light to check the size of the air cell and yolk position), and <strong>shaking</strong> the egg (a fresh egg does not make a sloshing sound).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Methods of Cooking Eggs</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Eggs can be boiled (soft or hard), fried, poached, scrambled, or baked. Overcooking eggs at very high heat toughens the protein and can cause a greenish ring around a hard-boiled yolk (due to iron and sulphur compounds reacting).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC frequently asks about the water test for freshness: a fresh egg sinks and lies horizontally at the bottom of a bowl of water because its air cell is small; a stale egg floats or stands upright because its air cell has enlarged.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> An egg consists of shell, air cell, albumen, yolk, chalazae and vitelline membrane. It is a rich source of first-class protein and vitamins A, D, E, B12, and iron. Freshness is tested by the water test, candling, or shaking.\n</div>\n",
