    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Milk</span> is often called a \"complete food\" because it contains most of the nutrients the body needs, though not in perfectly balanced amounts. It is a highly perishable food, so proper handling, preservation and use in cookery are important.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Milk</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Milk is the fluid secreted by the mammary glands of mammals to feed their young, and is consumed by humans mainly from cows, goats, and, less commonly, other animals. Types available include fresh (liquid) milk, evaporated milk, condensed milk, powdered (dried) milk, and fermented milk products.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutritive Value of Milk</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Present in Milk</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protein</td><td>First-class protein (casein, whey)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fat</td><td>Present, varies with milk type (whole vs skimmed)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Carbohydrate</td><td>Lactose (milk sugar)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Calcium & Phosphorus</td><td>Rich source, important for bones and teeth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamins</td><td>A, D, and some B vitamins (especially B2/riboflavin)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamin C & Iron</td><td>Poor source of both</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Methods of Preserving Milk</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Because raw milk spoils quickly, it is preserved by: <strong>pasteurisation</strong> (heating to destroy harmful bacteria while retaining nutrients), <strong>sterilisation</strong> (higher heat for longer shelf life), <strong>evaporation</strong> (removing some water, e.g. evaporated milk), <strong>condensing</strong> (removing water and adding sugar, e.g. condensed milk), and <strong>drying</strong> (removing all moisture to produce powdered milk).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Examples of Milk Dishes and Products</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Milk is used to make yoghurt, cheese, butter, custard, milk puddings, and as an ingredient in cakes, sauces, and beverages. Fermented milk products like yoghurt result from the action of beneficial bacteria on lactose, producing lactic acid.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often distinguishes pasteurisation from sterilisation: pasteurisation uses moderate heat to kill harmful bacteria while preserving flavour and most nutrients (shorter shelf life); sterilisation uses higher heat for longer shelf life but affects flavour and some vitamins more.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Milk is a near-complete food, rich in protein, calcium, and vitamins A and D, but poor in iron and vitamin C. It is highly perishable and preserved by pasteurisation, sterilisation, evaporation, condensing, or drying.\n</div>\n",
