    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Sea foods</span> (also called fish and seafood) are aquatic animals eaten as food. They are an important and often more affordable source of first-class protein compared to meat, and are widely consumed across Nigeria's coastal and inland communities.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Classification of Sea Foods</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Oily/fatty fish</td><td>Mackerel, herring, sardine, tuna</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>White (lean) fish</td><td>Cod, tilapia, croaker</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shellfish (crustaceans)</td><td>Crab, shrimp, lobster</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shellfish (molluscs)</td><td>Oyster, snail, periwinkle, clam</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutritive Value of Sea Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Sea foods are rich in first-class protein, easy to digest, and generally lower in fat than red meat (except oily fish, which is rich in healthy omega-3 fats). They also supply iodine, phosphorus, and, in oily fish, vitamins A and D.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors to Consider When Choosing Sea Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Freshness (bright eyes, red gills, firm flesh, fresh smell, no sliminess), size, cost, availability, and intended method of cooking should all be considered.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Methods of Cooking Fish</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fish is naturally tender with little connective tissue, so it cooks quickly by almost any method: frying, grilling, steaming, poaching, baking, or stewing. Overcooking easily dries out and toughens fish because it has less fat and connective tissue to protect it than red meat.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks for signs of fresh fish: bright, clear (not sunken) eyes; red/pink gills; firm flesh that springs back when pressed; a fresh sea smell (not a strong \"fishy\"/ammonia smell); and scales that are firmly attached.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Sea foods include oily fish, white fish, crustaceans and molluscs. They are rich in first-class protein, easily digested, and cook quickly due to low connective tissue — but overcooking easily dries them out.\n</div>\n",
