    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Food additives</span> are substances added to food to improve flavour, appearance, texture, or shelf life. While some additives occur naturally, others are manufactured, and understanding their types and safe use is important for a health-conscious cook.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Food Additives</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A food additive is any substance not normally consumed as food itself but added intentionally to food during preparation, processing, or storage to achieve a technical purpose such as flavouring, colouring, preserving, or thickening.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Food Additives</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Flavourings</td><td>Enhance or add taste/aroma</td><td>Vanilla, seasoning cubes, curry, thyme</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Herbs</td><td>Add flavour from leafy plants</td><td>Basil, bay leaf, scent leaf</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Spices</td><td>Add flavour from seeds, bark, roots</td><td>Ginger, pepper, cloves, nutmeg</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Colourings</td><td>Improve or restore colour</td><td>Caramel colour, food dyes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Preservatives</td><td>Extend shelf life, prevent spoilage</td><td>Salt, sugar, vinegar, sodium benzoate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Thickeners/Stabilisers</td><td>Improve texture and consistency</td><td>Cornflour, gelatine, pectin</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Classification by Source</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Additives may be <strong>local (natural)</strong> — such as pepper, ginger, garlic, locust beans (iru), or ogiri — or <strong>foreign (synthetic/manufactured)</strong> — such as artificial seasoning cubes, food colourings, and chemical preservatives.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Uses and Precautions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Additives improve flavour, appearance, and shelf life of food, but excessive use — especially of artificial additives — can pose health risks. Additives should always be used in approved, moderate amounts, and food labels should be checked for permitted additives.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish herbs (leafy parts of plants used for flavour, e.g., basil, scent leaf) from spices (non-leafy parts like seeds, roots, bark — e.g., ginger, cloves, pepper). This distinction is a common WAEC question.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food additives (flavourings, herbs, spices, colourings, preservatives, thickeners) improve food quality and shelf life. They may be local/natural or foreign/synthetic, and should always be used in safe, moderate amounts.\n</div>\n",
