    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Starchy roots and plantain</span> are important energy-giving (carbohydrate) foods that form the basis of many Nigerian staple dishes. This topic covers their types, nutritive value, and culinary uses.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Starchy Roots</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Starchy roots are underground plant parts (roots or tubers) rich in starch, used as staple energy foods. Common examples in Nigeria include yam, cassava, cocoyam, and sweet potato.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutritive Value of Starchy Roots</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Starchy roots are primarily sources of carbohydrate (energy), with small amounts of protein, vitamin C, and dietary fibre (especially when eaten with the skin). They contain very little fat.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types and Value of Plantain</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Plantain is a starchy fruit related to bananas, eaten cooked (unlike the sweeter dessert banana). It exists in stages: unripe (green, very starchy, low sugar), ripe (yellow, sweeter, some sugar converted from starch), and overripe (very sweet, softer). Plantain provides carbohydrate, some vitamin A (especially in ripe plantain), vitamin C, and potassium.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Main Nutrient</th><th>Common Dishes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Yam</td><td>Carbohydrate (starch)</td><td>Pounded yam, boiled yam, yam porridge</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cassava</td><td>Carbohydrate (starch)</td><td>Garri, fufu, tapioca</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cocoyam</td><td>Carbohydrate (starch)</td><td>Cocoyam porridge, ekpang</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sweet potato</td><td>Carbohydrate, some vitamin A</td><td>Boiled/roasted sweet potato</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plantain</td><td>Carbohydrate, potassium</td><td>Fried plantain (dodo), boiled plantain, plantain porridge</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Dishes Made from Plantain</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Plantain is versatile: unripe plantain is boiled, roasted, or made into flour; ripe plantain is fried (dodo), roasted (boli), or cooked into porridge or pottage with other ingredients.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Note that cassava contains cyanogenic compounds (naturally occurring cyanide-related substances) that must be removed through processing (soaking, fermenting, drying) before safe consumption, especially in raw or bitter cassava varieties.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Starchy roots (yam, cassava, cocoyam, sweet potato) and plantain are key carbohydrate/energy staples in Nigerian diets, with modest protein, vitamin, and fibre content, and are prepared in many popular local dishes.\n</div>\n",
