    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Special nutritional needs</span> refers to how nutrient and meal-planning requirements differ across life stages. This first part focuses on infants, children, and adolescents, whose bodies have distinct growth and developmental demands.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Nutritional Needs of Infants (0–1 year)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Infants need breast milk (or infant formula) exclusively for about the first six months, as it provides ideal nutrition and antibodies. From about six months, nutrient-dense complementary foods (mashed fruits, cereals, pureed vegetables) are gradually introduced alongside continued breastfeeding, since infants have small stomachs but high energy and protein needs relative to body weight for rapid growth.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutritional Needs of Children (1–12 years)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Children need diets rich in protein (for growth), calcium and vitamin D (for bone/teeth development), iron (to prevent anaemia and support cognitive development), and adequate energy for their high activity levels. Meals should be varied, colourful, and appropriately portioned, avoiding excessive sugary or fatty snacks.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutritional Needs of Adolescents (13–19 years)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Adolescence is a period of rapid growth (growth spurt) and puberty, requiring increased energy, protein, calcium, iron (especially for girls due to menstruation), and zinc. Poor nutrition at this stage can affect growth, academic performance, and long-term health.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Key Nutritional Priorities</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Infants</td><td>Breast milk/formula; iron-rich complementary foods from 6 months</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Children</td><td>Protein, calcium, vitamin D, iron; balanced varied meals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Adolescents</td><td>Increased energy, protein, calcium, iron (esp. girls), zinc</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Meal Planning Considerations</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Meal plans for these groups should consider appetite, food preferences, nutrient density (nutrients per portion, since young stomachs are small), and the need to avoid deficiencies during rapid growth periods.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks why exclusive breastfeeding is recommended for the first six months: breast milk provides ideal nutrition, easily digestible protein, and antibodies that protect the infant from infections.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Infants need breast milk then nutrient-dense complementary foods; children need balanced diets rich in protein, calcium and iron; adolescents need increased energy, protein, calcium, iron and zinc to support rapid growth and puberty.\n</div>\n",
