    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Special nutritional needs</span> continues here with adults, the aged, and pregnant and lactating mothers — groups whose nutrient requirements are shaped by maintenance, ageing, or the demands of pregnancy and breastfeeding.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Nutritional Needs of Adults</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Adults need a balanced diet to maintain body functions, prevent excess weight gain, and reduce the risk of diet-related diseases (e.g., hypertension, diabetes). Energy needs depend on activity level, and a mix of all nutrient classes in moderate amounts supports long-term health.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutritional Needs of the Aged (Elderly)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The elderly generally need fewer calories (due to reduced activity and slower metabolism) but still require adequate protein (to prevent muscle loss), calcium and vitamin D (to protect against osteoporosis), fibre (for digestive health), and easily chewable/digestible foods, as dental and digestive function may decline with age.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutritional Needs of Pregnant Women</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Pregnancy increases the need for folic acid (to prevent neural tube defects, especially early in pregnancy), iron (to support increased blood volume and prevent anaemia), calcium (for fetal bone development), and additional protein and energy to support fetal growth.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Nutritional Needs of Lactating (Breastfeeding) Mothers</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Breastfeeding mothers need extra energy, protein, calcium, and fluids to support milk production, alongside continued good nutrition to replenish nutrient stores used during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Key Nutritional Priorities</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Adults</td><td>Balanced diet; moderation; disease prevention</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Aged</td><td>Fewer calories; adequate protein, calcium, vitamin D, fibre; easily digestible foods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pregnant women</td><td>Folic acid, iron, calcium, extra protein/energy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lactating mothers</td><td>Extra energy, protein, calcium, fluids</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks why folic acid is emphasised in early pregnancy — it helps prevent neural tube defects (such as spina bifida) in the developing fetus, so intake is especially important before and during early pregnancy.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Adults need balanced, moderate diets for maintenance; the aged need fewer calories but adequate protein, calcium and fibre; pregnant women need extra folic acid, iron and calcium; lactating mothers need extra energy, protein, calcium and fluids.\n</div>\n",
