    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Test interpretation</span> skills help students understand exactly what an exam question is asking and how to structure a correct, well-organised response — a critical skill for success in WAEC and other examinations.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Understanding Command Words</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Exam questions use specific command words that indicate the type of answer expected:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Command Word</th><th>What It Requires</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Define/State</td><td>Give a precise, concise meaning</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>List/Mention</td><td>Give items without detailed explanation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Explain/Describe</td><td>Give details and reasons, not just a list</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Differentiate/Distinguish</td><td>Show clear differences between two or more things</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Discuss</td><td>Examine a topic in detail, considering different points</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enumerate</td><td>List points systematically, often numbered</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Reading and Analysing the Question</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Before answering, read the question carefully to identify: the command word, the specific topic/subject being asked about, the number of points required (e.g., \"list five...\"), and any conditions (e.g., \"with reference to...\").</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Planning and Structuring a Response</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A good response should be well organised: a brief introduction (where relevant), clearly presented points (numbered or in paragraphs, matching the command word), and a logical order. For \"list\" questions, provide exactly the number of points requested, no more, no less.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Common errors include: listing points when an explanation is required (or vice versa), writing beyond or below the words/points needed, misreading the topic, and failing to use correct subject terminology.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always underline or note the command word and the number of points required before answering. A \"list five factors\" question loses marks if you write only three, or if you explain each in a long paragraph instead of listing them.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Interpreting exam questions correctly means identifying the command word, understanding what type of answer it requires, planning a well-structured response, and avoiding common mistakes like ignoring instructions on quantity or depth.\n</div>\n",
