    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Flour cookery</span> covers the meaning, sources, and uses of flour in preparing dishes such as bread and pastries. Flour is a staple ingredient in many baked and cooked dishes worldwide, including Nigeria.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Flour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Flour is a fine powder obtained by grinding grains, seeds, or roots. Wheat flour is the most common type used in baking due to its gluten content, but local flours are also produced from other sources.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source</th><th>Common Use</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Wheat flour</td><td>Wheat grain</td><td>Bread, cakes, pastries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cassava flour</td><td>Cassava root</td><td>Local swallow, baking blends</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Yam flour (elubo)</td><td>Yam</td><td>Amala</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plantain flour</td><td>Unripe plantain</td><td>Amala/swallow</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maize flour</td><td>Maize/corn</td><td>Local pap, baked goods</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Local Sources of Flour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In Nigeria, flour is also produced locally from cassava, yam, plantain, maize, and other staples, providing more affordable alternatives to imported wheat flour, especially for traditional dishes.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutritive Value of Flour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Flour is primarily a source of carbohydrate (starch), with some protein (especially wheat flour, due to gluten), and small amounts of B-vitamins and minerals depending on how refined the flour is (whole grain flours retain more fibre and nutrients than refined white flour).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Dishes from Flour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Wheat flour is used to make bread, cakes, biscuits, pastries (meat pie, puff-puff, doughnuts), and pasta. Local flours are used for traditional swallow dishes such as amala.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often distinguishes wheat flour from local flours by noting that wheat flour's gluten content gives dough its elastic, stretchy quality — essential for bread and pastry structure, which most local flours lack.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Flour, obtained by grinding grains, roots, or seeds, is mainly a carbohydrate food. Wheat flour (with gluten) is key for bread and pastries, while local flours (cassava, yam, plantain, maize) support traditional Nigerian dishes.\n</div>\n",
