    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Raising agents</span> are substances that introduce or produce gas within a dough or batter, causing it to rise and become light and porous. They are essential in baking bread, cakes, and many pastries.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Raising Agents</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A raising agent is any substance or method that introduces gas (usually carbon dioxide or air) into a mixture, causing it to expand and become light in texture during cooking.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Raising Agents</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Biological</td><td>Living micro-organism (yeast) ferments sugar, producing CO2 gas</td><td>Yeast</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Chemical reaction releases CO2 gas</td><td>Baking powder, baking soda (bicarbonate of soda)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mechanical/Physical</td><td>Air is trapped by beating, whisking, or sieving; steam expands during cooking</td><td>Whisked eggs, creaming, steam in choux pastry</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Composition and Action</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Yeast</strong> is a living micro-organism that, given warmth, moisture, and sugar, ferments and releases carbon dioxide gas, causing dough to rise slowly (used in bread). <strong>Baking powder</strong> is a mixture of an acid and a base (usually bicarbonate of soda plus a weak acid, such as cream of tartar), which reacts with moisture/heat to release carbon dioxide quickly. <strong>Baking soda (bicarbonate of soda)</strong> alone requires an acidic ingredient (like lemon juice or buttermilk) in the recipe to activate and release gas.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Evaluation of Raising Agents</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Yeast gives bread its characteristic flavour and open texture but requires time to ferment. Chemical raising agents act faster, suited to cakes and quick breads, but can leave a slightly bitter or metallic aftertaste if used excessively.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC frequently asks students to classify raising agents as biological, chemical, or mechanical/physical, and to explain how each works. Remember: yeast = biological (fermentation); baking powder/soda = chemical (acid-base reaction); whisked air/steam = mechanical/physical.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Raising agents (yeast, baking powder, baking soda, and mechanical methods like whisking or steam) introduce gas into dough/batter, making baked goods light and porous. They are classified as biological, chemical, or mechanical/physical.\n</div>\n",
