    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Convenience foods</span> are foods that have been partly or fully prepared before purchase, saving the cook time and effort. They are increasingly popular with busy households and understanding their forms, benefits, and drawbacks is important for informed meal planning.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Convenience Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Convenience foods are foods that have undergone some degree of pre-preparation (washing, cutting, cooking, packaging) by a manufacturer before reaching the consumer, reducing the time and effort needed at home.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Forms and Examples of Convenience Foods</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Ready-to-eat</td><td>Bread, biscuits, canned fish</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partly prepared</td><td>Frozen chopped vegetables, pre-marinated meat</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Instant/quick-cook</td><td>Instant noodles, instant pap, powdered soup mix</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Canned/tinned foods</td><td>Canned beans, tomatoes, sardines</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors to Consider When Selecting Convenience Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consider nutritional value (many are higher in salt, sugar or fat), cost compared to fresh alternatives, the sell-by/expiry date, packaging integrity, and how well they suit the intended dish.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Advantages and Disadvantages</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Advantages:</strong> saves time and effort, reduces food waste (portioned/preserved), often has a longer shelf life, and requires less skill to prepare. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> generally more expensive than raw ingredients per unit, may contain more salt, sugar, fat, or additives, and can be less fresh/nutritious than home-prepared alternatives.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Dishes Made with Convenience Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Convenience foods can be used creatively — e.g., canned tomatoes in stew, frozen vegetables in fried rice, instant noodles enhanced with fresh vegetables and egg — to save time while still producing a nutritious meal.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to weigh advantages against disadvantages of convenience foods — remember the trade-off: time-saving and longer shelf life versus higher cost and often lower nutritional quality (more salt/sugar/fat/additives).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Convenience foods are partly or fully pre-prepared foods (ready-to-eat, instant, canned) that save time but often cost more and may be less nutritious than fresh alternatives. Selection should weigh cost, nutrition, and freshness/expiry.\n</div>\n",
