    "module": "Food Preservation & Storage",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Food preservation</span> refers to methods used to protect food from spoilage, extending its usability and safety. Understanding why food spoils and why preservation matters is the foundation for effective food handling.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Spoilage</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Food spoilage is the deterioration of food's quality, safety, or nutritional value, making it unfit or unsafe to eat. Spoilage can be detected through changes in colour, smell, texture, or taste.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Causes and Agents of Spoilage</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>How It Causes Spoilage</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Micro-organisms (bacteria, moulds, yeasts)</td><td>Multiply in food, breaking it down and producing toxins</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enzymes</td><td>Naturally present in food; continue to ripen/break down food after harvest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Oxygen (oxidation)</td><td>Causes rancidity in fats, browning in cut fruit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moisture/heat/light</td><td>Encourage microbial growth and chemical reactions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pests (insects, rodents)</td><td>Physically damage and contaminate food</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Food Preservation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Preservation reduces food waste, ensures a steady food supply throughout the year (including off-season), saves money, allows storage of surplus harvest, and reduces the risk of foodborne illness from spoiled food.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Principles of Food Preservation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Preservation methods generally work by: removing or inactivating micro-organisms/enzymes (heat treatment), removing moisture they need to grow (drying), lowering temperature to slow their activity (chilling/freezing), creating unfavourable conditions (high salt/sugar/acid concentration), or excluding air (vacuum sealing, canning).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to state the causes of food spoilage — remember the key agents: micro-organisms, enzymes, oxygen, moisture, and pests. Most preservation methods target one or more of these.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food spoilage results from micro-organisms, enzymes, oxygen, moisture, and pests. Preservation is important for reducing waste, ensuring food supply, and food safety, and works by controlling these spoilage agents.\n</div>\n",
