    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Methods of food preservation</span> are the specific practical techniques — such as drying, smoking, salting, freezing, and canning — used to protect food from spoilage and extend its shelf life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Drying (Dehydration)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Drying removes moisture from food (sun-drying or artificial dehydration), preventing microbial growth. Used for fish, meat, grains, pepper, and vegetables.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Smoking</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Smoking exposes food (commonly fish and meat) to smoke from burning wood, which dries the food, adds antimicrobial compounds from the smoke, and imparts flavour, extending shelf life.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Salting (Curing)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Salting draws moisture out of food through osmosis, creating conditions unfavourable for microbial growth. Commonly used for fish and meat.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Freezing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Freezing lowers food temperature below 0°C, slowing microbial activity and enzyme action almost to a halt, preserving food for extended periods, especially meat, fish, and some vegetables.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Canning</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Canning involves sealing food in airtight cans or jars and heat-treating it to destroy micro-organisms, then sealing it to prevent recontamination — giving a long shelf life at room temperature.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Principle</th><th>Common Foods</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Drying</td><td>Removes moisture</td><td>Fish, meat, grains, pepper</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Smoking</td><td>Dries + antimicrobial smoke compounds</td><td>Fish, meat</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Salting</td><td>Draws out moisture via osmosis</td><td>Fish, meat</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Freezing</td><td>Low temperature halts microbial activity</td><td>Meat, fish, vegetables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Canning</td><td>Heat treatment + airtight seal</td><td>Beans, tomatoes, fish, fruits</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to explain the principle behind a named preservation method — always link the method to how it controls spoilage agents (removing moisture, excluding air, lowering temperature, or destroying micro-organisms with heat).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food preservation methods include drying, smoking, salting, freezing, and canning, each working by removing moisture, adding antimicrobial elements, lowering temperature, or destroying micro-organisms through heat and airtight sealing.\n</div>\n",
