    "topic": "Food Storage",
    "topicCode": "SS2-FDN-20",
    "module": "Food Preservation & Storage",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Food storage</span> is the safe and appropriate keeping of food after purchase, preparation, or preservation, to maintain its quality and safety until it is needed. Correct storage practices work hand-in-hand with preservation to reduce spoilage and waste.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Importance of Food Storage</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Food storage means keeping food under suitable conditions (temperature, humidity, container type) to maintain quality and prevent spoilage or contamination until the food is used. Proper storage reduces waste, saves money, and protects health.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Storage of Perishable Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Perishable foods (fresh meat, fish, milk, vegetables) spoil quickly and need refrigeration or freezing, along with prompt use. Raw meat/fish should be stored separately from ready-to-eat food to prevent cross-contamination.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Storage of Non-Perishable Foods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Non-perishable foods (grains, dried beans, flour, canned goods) can be stored at room temperature in a cool, dry, well-ventilated place, protected from pests, moisture, and direct sunlight.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Storage Equipment and Spaces</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Storage Type</th><th>Suitable For</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Refrigerator</td><td>Perishables needing short-term cold storage (few days)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deep freezer</td><td>Perishables for long-term storage (weeks to months)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Barns/silos</td><td>Bulk storage of grains at farm or community level</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Store cupboards/pantries</td><td>Dry, non-perishable goods (canned food, flour, spices)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Airtight containers</td><td>Protecting dry foods from moisture and pests</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. General Storage Guidelines</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Label and date stored food where possible, follow \"first in, first out\" (use older stock first), avoid overcrowding storage spaces (allows air circulation), and regularly check for signs of spoilage or pest infestation.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to differentiate storage needs of perishable versus non-perishable foods — remember: perishables need cold storage and prompt use; non-perishables need cool, dry, pest-free storage at room temperature.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food storage involves keeping perishable foods refrigerated/frozen and non-perishable foods in cool, dry, pest-free conditions, using appropriate equipment (fridge, freezer, barns, pantries) to maintain quality and prevent spoilage.\n</div>\n",
