    "topic": "Budgeting",
    "topicCode": "SS2-FDN-22",
    "module": "Home & Consumer Economics",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Budgeting</span> is the process of planning how income will be spent on needs and wants over a period of time. In Food and Nutrition, budgeting focuses especially on planning family food expenditure wisely.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Budgeting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A budget is a financial plan that shows expected income and planned expenditure over a given period (e.g., weekly or monthly), helping ensure money is used wisely and does not run out before needs are met.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Income and Expenditure</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Income</strong> is money received (e.g., salary, wages, allowances, business profit). <strong>Expenditure</strong> is money spent, divided into <strong>fixed expenditure</strong> (rent, school fees — regular, unavoidable) and <strong>variable expenditure</strong> (food, clothing, entertainment — amounts that can be adjusted).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Family Needs and Food Budgeting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A family budget should prioritise essential needs (food, shelter, healthcare, education) before wants (luxuries, entertainment). Food budgeting specifically involves planning how much of the family income is allocated to food, balancing nutrition, family size, and cost.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Factors Influencing Budgeting</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Influence</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Family income</td><td>Determines the overall amount available to spend</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Family size and composition</td><td>More members/growing children increase food needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Price of goods</td><td>Rising prices (inflation) reduce purchasing power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Family needs and priorities</td><td>Health conditions, education costs, etc.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Season/availability</td><td>Seasonal foods are often cheaper</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of Budgeting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Budgeting helps avoid overspending, ensures essential needs are met, allows for savings, helps track spending patterns, and reduces financial stress.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to distinguish fixed from variable expenditure — remember: fixed expenditure (like rent) stays the same and must be paid regardless; variable expenditure (like food, clothing) can be adjusted based on available income.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Budgeting is planning income against expenditure, balancing fixed and variable costs and prioritising essential family needs (especially food) over wants, influenced by income, family size, prices, and needs.\n</div>\n",
