    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Food purchasing</span> involves the methods and decisions used to buy food efficiently and economically. Understanding different buying strategies helps a household get the best value while avoiding waste.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Food Purchasing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Food purchasing is the process of selecting and buying food items to meet household needs, considering cost, quality, quantity, and how the food will be used.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Methods of Food Purchasing</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Consideration</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bulk purchasing</td><td>Buying large quantities at once, often at a lower unit price</td><td>Requires storage space and upfront capital</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Piece-meal purchasing</td><td>Buying small quantities as needed, item by item</td><td>Convenient for perishables; often costs more per unit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Impulse buying</td><td>Unplanned purchase made on the spot, without prior intention</td><td>Can lead to overspending and waste</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors to Consider When Purchasing Food</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consider budget, freshness/quality, quantity needed, storage capacity at home, price comparisons between sellers, and the intended use (immediate meal vs. stock for later).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Advantages and Disadvantages</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Bulk purchasing</strong> saves money per unit and reduces shopping trips but requires storage space and more upfront money, and risks spoilage if not preserved properly. <strong>Piece-meal purchasing</strong> ensures freshness and matches immediate needs but can be more expensive per unit and time-consuming. <strong>Impulse buying</strong> is generally discouraged, as it undermines budgeting and can lead to unnecessary spending.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Preparing a Shopping List</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A shopping list — prepared based on planned meals, checked against what is already available at home, and organised by category — helps avoid impulse buying, saves time, and keeps purchasing within budget.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often asks students to compare bulk purchasing and piece-meal purchasing. Remember: bulk = cheaper per unit but needs storage and upfront cash; piece-meal = fresher and flexible but costs more per unit over time.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food purchasing methods include bulk buying, piece-meal buying, and impulse buying. Careful planning, price comparison, and a shopping list help a household purchase food economically while minimising waste and overspending.\n</div>\n",
