    "topic": "Consumer Education I & II",
    "topicCode": "SS2-FDN-24",
    "module": "Home & Consumer Economics",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Consumer education</span> equips individuals with the knowledge and skills to make informed, safe purchasing decisions, understand their rights, and recognise unfair or unsafe trade practices in the marketplace.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Principles of Consumer Education</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumer education teaches individuals how to be informed, responsible buyers — understanding product quality, pricing, labelling, and their rights and responsibilities as consumers in the marketplace.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Consumer Agents and Government Agencies</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agency</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>NAFDAC (National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control)</td><td>Regulates and controls the quality, safety, and registration of food, drugs, and related products</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SON (Standards Organisation of Nigeria)</td><td>Sets and enforces national quality standards for products</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>FCCPC (Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Commission)</td><td>Protects consumer rights, addresses unfair trade practices and complaints</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Duties of Consumer Agents</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumer protection agencies inspect and regulate products, enforce quality/safety standards, investigate consumer complaints, penalise businesses engaging in unfair practices, and educate the public on consumer rights.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Unfair Consumer Practices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Adulteration</strong> is mixing a genuine product with inferior or harmful substances to increase quantity/profit (e.g., diluting milk with water). <strong>Falsification</strong> is presenting false information about a product (e.g., fake certification). <strong>Misbranding</strong> is labelling a product inaccurately or misleadingly (e.g., wrong weight, false expiry date, or false health claims).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC frequently asks students to define and distinguish adulteration, falsification, and misbranding, and to name relevant consumer protection agencies (NAFDAC, SON, FCCPC) and their roles.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Consumer education helps individuals make informed purchases and understand their rights. Agencies like NAFDAC, SON, and FCCPC protect consumers from unfair practices such as adulteration, falsification, and misbranding.\n</div>\n",
